   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to the manufacturing and sale of imitation birch and walnut syrups and the branding and labeling of such syrups",
    "basePrintNo": "S3669",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3669-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§203 & 204, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3669\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in\nrelation to the manufacturing and sale of imitation birch and walnut\nsyrups and the branding and labeling of such syrups\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo establish purity and quality requirements in law for birch and\nwalnut syrup and sugars similar to maple.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 203 of the Agriculture and Markets Law by\nprohibiting imitation birch and walnut sugar and syrup. The section\nalso defines the terms, \"maple sugar,\" and\"birch sugar.\"\n\nSection 2 amends section 204 of the Agriculture and Markets Law\nrelated to the branding and labeling of birch and walnut sugar and\nsyrup mixtures.\n\nSection 3 provides the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York is an international leader in the production and sale of high\nquality maple syrup and sugars. The hundreds of entrepreneurs who\nproduce this product contribute millions of dollars to the upstate\neconomy and help preserve valuable forests. They also provide a\npopular product with New York families and food businesses.\n\nMaple production has a very short season that is highly vulnerable to\nbad weather and other environmental factors. Cornell University has\nbeen working with maple producers to help them develop additional\nproducts that would build on the expertise and equipment already used\nfor maple. As a result of those efforts, birch and walnut are two\nother species of trees that are being tapped by producers to expand\ntheir markets for syrups and sugars. To help these new produces\nsucceed, state standards and regulations are needed to protect product\nidentity. This will help producers comply with regulations, protect\nlegitimate businesses from unfair competition, and protect consumers\nfrom buying lesser quality products.\n\nThis legislation would add birch and walnut to the existing section of\nlaw relating to maple purity and labeling. It would allow the\nDepartment of Agriculture and Markets to regulate these newer syrups\nin the same manner as maple syrup. This will include providing\nexemptions from fees that might be a barrier to entering this market.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n\n",
